\section{Memory Implementation}
\subsection{Entity Design}

The memory implementation presented interesting architectural choices. While conceptually straightforward, the design required careful consideration of access methodologies:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Memory Access Strategy}: 
    I initially implemented a \textbf{synchronous read/write} design with dual ports (\texttt{read\_addr} and \texttt{write\_addr}) and separate data registers (\texttt{mem\_data\_in} and \texttt{mem\_data\_out}). This approach worked correctly but was later modified to use \textbf{asynchronous/combinational reads} to better align with the decoder's timing requirements. The final implementation drives \texttt{data\_out} immediately when \texttt{read\_en} is asserted.

    \begin{lstlisting}[language=VHDL, caption={Continuous assignment of data\_out enabling combinational reading}]
-- Combinational read
data_out <= mem(to_integer(unsigned(read_addr))) when read_en ='1' 
            else (others => '0'); 
    \end{lstlisting}

    \item \textbf{Status Register Handling}:
    The special case of the status register at address \texttt{0x03} required particular attention. The design prioritizes top-level \texttt{data\_in} over \texttt{status\_in} during writes to this address. Additionally, I introduced a \texttt{status\_write\_enable} signal to properly handle bit-set (BSF) and bit-clear (BCF) operations. This refinement emerged during debugging, demonstrating the iterative nature of hardware design and verification.

    \begin{lstlisting}[language=VHDL, caption={Use of status\_write\_enable}]
elsif (status_write_enable) then --for RLF and RRF
    mem(to_integer(unsigned(STATUS_ADDR))) <= 
        (DATA_WIDTH-1 downto 3 => '0') & mem_status_in;
    report "RLF instruction here";
end if;
    \end{lstlisting}
\end{itemize}

