\section{Decoder}

To implement the decoder, I started reading the datasheet thoroughly. Before that, I only read it for the ALU to check what operations I needed to implement. I understood how to execute each instruction using the four different states: \texttt{iFetch}, \texttt{Mread}, \texttt{Execute} and \texttt{Mwrite}. I understood how instructions were thought: most of the instructions have an opcode so we can identify the operation, an address or a literal coded with a certain number of bits. Thanks to this study, I drew a simple finite state machine which is not complete (so I won't submit it here). I just used it for myself in order to have clearer ideas on how to start coding.

At first, I thought the decoder didn't need several outputs/inputs except \texttt{clk}, \texttt{rst}, \texttt{instruction} and \texttt{pc}. But then, when I first simulated my decoder I thought I couldn't access internal signals but I just forgot that GTKWave enables you to do that by clicking on the module in the GTKWave graphic interface. I remembered this only after implementing a decoder with more outputs and inputs, but this is not important because choosing what are the outputs of the decoder is a "choice" and since we don't have any criteria to respect, I decided to keep it like that. All signals used by submodules memory and ALU are also outputs of the decoder. That way, it's also easy for me to select these signals when launching GTKWave because I don't have to go through the submodules.

\subsection{Instruction Package}
The first thing I decided to do before implementing the decoder is a package named \texttt{instruction\_pkg} that contains a new type definition: \texttt{instruction\_type}. I use this type to make the difference between:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Byte-oriented
    \item Bit-oriented
    \item Literal and control (\texttt{lANDc})
    \item Special operations (\texttt{CALL}, \texttt{GOTO})
\end{itemize}

In this package I also define a procedure called \texttt{data\_extraction} that:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Assigns to \texttt{operation} the type of operation executed by the instruction (given as an input of the procedure)
    \item Extracts the rest of the data from each instruction
\end{itemize}

\begin{lstlisting}[language=VHDL,caption={Beginning of instruction package}]
package instruction_pkg is
    -- Instruction type enumeration
    type instruction_type is (byte_oriented, bit_oriented, lANDc, CLRW, special, unknown);
    
    -- Constants for special instructions
    constant NOP_INSTRUCTION : std_logic_vector(13 downto 0) := "00000000000000";
    constant RETURN_INSTRUCTION : std_logic_vector(13 downto 0) := "00000000001000";
    constant ADDR_WIDTH : integer := 7;
    constant DATA_WIDTH : integer := 8;
    
    -- Data extraction procedure
    procedure data_extraction (
        -- Input
        signal instruction : in std_logic_vector(13 downto 0);
        -- Global output
        signal operation : out instruction_type;
        -- Output byte oriented
        signal opcode_byte : out std_logic_vector(5 downto 0); -- 6 bits
        signal d_byte : out std_logic;
        signal f_byte : out std_logic_vector(6 downto 0);
        -- Output bit-oriented
        signal opcode_bit : out std_logic_vector(3 downto 0); -- 4 bits
        signal b_bit : out std_logic_vector(2 downto 0);
        signal f_bit : out std_logic_vector(6 downto 0);
        -- Output literal and control
        signal opcode_lANDc : out std_logic_vector(5 downto 0); -- 6 bits
        signal k_literal : out std_logic_vector(7 downto 0);
        -- Output special
        signal opcode_special : out std_logic_vector(2 downto 0);
        signal k_special : out std_logic_vector(10 downto 0);
        -- Memory interface
        signal mem_read_address : out std_logic_vector(6 downto 0)
    );
end package instruction_pkg;

\end{lstlisting}



For instance, byte-oriented operations are recognized because they all start by "00". We then assign:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The opcode to \texttt{opcode\_byte}
    \item The direction to \texttt{d\_byte}
    \item The 7-bit address to \texttt{mem\_read\_address} (used during reading)
\end{itemize}

I call this procedure in \texttt{iFetch} and it makes my code easier to read. The process of extracting data is purely combinational, so the procedure is the right way to do this.

At first, this package also defined a type \texttt{state} which could be \texttt{iFetch}, \texttt{Mread}, \texttt{Execute} or \texttt{Mwrite}. But then when I first simulated the decoder, I couldn't see the signals \texttt{current\_state} and \texttt{next\_state} because GTKWave doesn't know how to read this new type. I should have used strings: "\texttt{iFetch}", etc. But I finally decided to use \texttt{std\_logic\_vector}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item "00" for \texttt{iFetch}
    \item "01" for \texttt{Mread}
    \item ...
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Module Connections}
In \texttt{decoder.vhd}, I instantiate ALU and memory using positional assignment and signals I used as output of my decoder. They are connected together this way:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{alu\_status\_out\_reg} is just a register that retains the value of \texttt{alu\_status\_out} when calculated. We assign \texttt{alu\_status\_out\_reg} to \texttt{mem\_status\_in} so the memory takes care of writing this status to address \texttt{0x03h}. This is taken care of in the memory implementation and detailed above.
    
    \item We then connect \texttt{mem\_status\_out} and \texttt{alu\_status\_in}. Anytime, the memory is assigning a value to \texttt{mem\_status\_out} which is the value it found at address \texttt{0x03h} and we use this as input for the ALU.
\end{itemize}

This part was a bit tricky. At first I connected \texttt{mem\_status\_in} and \texttt{alu\_status\_out} but I saw in the testbench that it wasn't the good solution because when not used, the ALU would always assign 0 for \texttt{alu\_status\_out} (it wasn't retaining its value). So I used a register to do this.

I will speak of \texttt{status\_write\_enable} later (last signal of memory outputs).

\subsection{State Machine Implementation}
I first implemented a synchronous process with asynchronous reset. I may change this functionality later. This process takes care of:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Assigning \texttt{next\_state} to \texttt{current\_state} each rising clock edge
    \item Updating \texttt{pc} when the current state is \texttt{Execute} (so that \texttt{pc} can be changed exactly at the end of the instruction)
\end{itemize}

At first, \texttt{pc} was always incremented but then I added an if statement that assigned a different value than \texttt{pc + 1} to \texttt{pc} if the operation was \texttt{CALL}, \texttt{GOTO}, \texttt{RETLW} or \texttt{RETURN}. This other value was \texttt{pc\_reg} and is assigned in the other process (I will speak about it later). This first process is very simple.

The second process takes care of the logic and describes a case statement which reacts according to \texttt{current\_state} and calculates \texttt{next\_state}. I will speak about things that I find important in this process or troubles I encountered and how I resolved them.

At first, I wanted to reset all outputs of the decoder but I figured that I only need to reset:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The memory contents (nothing to do because the memory is already linked with the top-module reset)
    \item \texttt{next\_state} because I had a problem when simulating it (I was blocked at state "01" after a reset which is not normal)
    \item The accumulation register \texttt{w\_register}
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{State Machine Details}
The first part of case statement is for fetching the instruction. It:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Calls the extraction procedure
    \item Determines if the instruction needs reading or not
    \item Chooses the \texttt{next\_state} which can be \texttt{Mread} or \texttt{Execute} based on instruction
    \item If the \texttt{next\_state} is reading, it will also assert \texttt{mem\_read\_en} which enables reading from memory address \texttt{mem\_read\_addr}
\end{itemize}

The second part of the case is for memory reading only. For operations that need reading from memory, it will keep the value read by assigning \texttt{mem\_data\_out} to \texttt{operand} which then will be used to feed the ALU. The next state is always \texttt{Execute} here.

The next part of case statement is for \texttt{Execute} state. Based on operation (\texttt{byte\_oriented}, \texttt{bit\_oriented}, \texttt{lANDc}, \texttt{special}...) and opcode (\texttt{opcode\_byte}, \texttt{opcode\_bit}, \texttt{opcode\_lANDc}, \texttt{opcode\_special}...), it determines how to feed the ALU properly using \texttt{operand}, \texttt{w\_register}, \texttt{k\_literal} or \texttt{k\_special}...

The last part of case statement is \texttt{Mwrite}. In this part:
\begin{itemize}
    \item I assign \texttt{alu\_status\_out} to \texttt{alu\_status\_out\_reg} for the reasons I explained earlier
    \item If operation is \texttt{BSF} or \texttt{BCF}, I assert \texttt{status\_write\_enable} that enables these operations to rotate properly through carry bit
\end{itemize}

I did this because when I simulated an \texttt{RRF} instruction it wasn't properly doing the operation wanted, so I decided to change the implementation of my memory by adding a \texttt{status\_enable} signal as input. When it is activated and \texttt{mem\_write\_enable} isn't asserted, it still writes the content of \texttt{status\_in} to \texttt{STATUS\_ADDR 0x03h}. Doing multiple simulations and testbenches helped me a lot to observe different errors and their causes and also to fix problems by trying different solutions.

The \texttt{next\_state} when memory writing is always \texttt{iFetch}.

An if statement is then taking care of writing \texttt{alu\_result} to the proper address or \texttt{w\_register} depending on \texttt{d} (if 1, result is written to address else to \texttt{w\_register}).

\subsection{Testing and Debugging}
With the testbench, I simulated ALL instructions one by one, including \texttt{CALL}, \texttt{GOTO}, \texttt{RETLW}, \texttt{RETURN}.

A problem arose when trying the instruction \texttt{ADDWF}. It was the first time I was reading an operand from the memory. The problem was that I was receiving the operand one cycle after asking for it (after asserting \texttt{mem\_read\_en}). I decided to implement combinational read because before this I was using a synchronous read. I just changed a bit my memory implementation and it worked.

I also had many problems with status when doing \texttt{RLF} and \texttt{RRF} instructions but I think I came to a good solution with \texttt{status\_write\_enable} and \texttt{status\_out\_reg} as I explained earlier. At first I was connecting \texttt{alu\_status\_out} to \texttt{mem\_status\_in} but the fact that \texttt{alu\_status\_out} was "000" except when the ALU was used provoked incorrect writing to status address \texttt{0x03h} in memory. That's when I decided to connect \texttt{alu\_status\_out\_reg} to \texttt{mem\_status\_in} instead of \texttt{alu\_status\_out}.

Once you read correctly from memory, write correctly to it and take care of status then most of operations worked. I just had to code \texttt{call}, \texttt{goto}, \texttt{retlw} and \texttt{return}.

In order to code them, I would assign \texttt{pc\_reg} in \texttt{Execute} depending on the operation asked. \texttt{pc\_reg} would then keep its value and I would assign \texttt{pc\_reg} to \texttt{pc} in first process when doing \texttt{call}, \texttt{goto}, \texttt{retlw} and \texttt{return}. I chose to do this because you can't drive \texttt{pc} with 2 different processes.

Simulations took most of my time when doing this because I wanted to do a complete testbench to not have problems later.

\section{Stack 8$\times$13}
I designed a simple stack with 8 levels of 13 bits each. Push and pop operations cannot be asserted simultaneously as this functionality was not required. The stack pointer is neither directly readable nor writable, and the output always reflects the top of the stack. This last feature allows me to read directly from the stack without enabling any signal, as it's combinational.

Following a TA's advice, I found existing stack implementation code online since many stack designs already exist. I modified a few parameters to match my specific requirements (number of levels, bit width, etc.).

For testing, I performed the following operations:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Pushing and popping a few elements
    \item Pushing until the stack was full
    \item Popping until it was empty
\end{itemize}

After testing multiple cases, I gained a thorough understanding of how the stack operates. Debugging the code helped me comprehend many aspects of its functionality. The implementation worked correctly without any problems.

\section{Connection Between Stack and Rest of the Logic}
I modified my decoder implementation to connect the stack properly. The stack handles push and pop operations for specific instructions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{CALL}: Pushes \texttt{pc+1} to the stack by placing the value on \texttt{stack\_data\_in} and asserting \texttt{push}
    \item \textbf{RETLW} and \textbf{RETURN}: Retrieves the top of stack (\texttt{stack\_data\_out}) to feed \texttt{pc\_reg}, then pops the stack by asserting \texttt{pop}
\end{itemize}

The decoder implementation was updated by:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Removing the \texttt{tos} (top of stack) signal
    \item Adding new decoder outputs: \texttt{push}, \texttt{pop}, \texttt{stack\_data\_in}, and \texttt{stack\_data\_out}
    \item Implementing the required control logic
\end{itemize}

These changes were incorporated into a new VHDL file named \texttt{pic\_rtl.vhdl}.

\subsection{Stack Limitations}
The 8-level stack allows for 8 nested subprogram calls. However, calling a 9th subprogram would overwrite the first return address, causing program errors. This represents a fundamental limitation of the stack depth.

